  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The spatial configurations rely on computational surrogate modeling and still require formal evaluation for real-world planning feasibility and zoning implementation constraints.",
      "what": "The study optimized Local Climate Zone (LCZ) spatial configurations across eight local administrative areas in Greater Kuala Lumpur for 1500 iterations using Genetic Algorithm (GA) and Particle Swarm Optimisation (PSO) coupled with a ResNet model to predict Surface Urban Heat Island intensity (SUHII). Maintaining population and GDP as non-decreasing constraints, PSO achieved a superior average extreme SUHII reduction of 3.03℃ while altering 7.95% of the total land area, whereas GA yielded a 2.85℃ reduction by altering 18.76% of the area. A Generative Adversarial Network was subsequently used to create 3D urban morphology blocks representing the optimized spatial layouts.",
      "why": "Urban planners gain a data-driven method to strategically reconfigure land-use zones for targeted cooling without sacrificing demographic or economic growth."
    }
  },
